# Preferences

None of these are pre-filled, and most are entirely optional. The
exception is `Operator:`: in a shared workspace (one with a
remote) it is required, and Hostwarden interviews you for it on
first use rather than filling it in silently (`rules/ssh-user.md`
→ Operator). Add a line to set any field:

- `Language: <name>` — e.g. `Language: German`.
- `Operator: <handle>` — a short handle; names you in journal
  entries and decisions.
- `Operator name: <full name>` — used for the email signature
  only.
- `Greeting: <text>` — the default close; override with your own
  (e.g. `Greeting: Viele Grüße\nHostwarden`).
- `Workspace remote: none` — the workspace stays on this
  machine, and no session asks about a remote
  (`rules/session-start.md` → The workspace remote).
- `Notice email: <address>` — the address a maintenance-window
  IT notice defaults to (`rules/maintenance-windows.md` →
  Telling IT).
- `SSH agent socket: <path>` — the local SSH agent for signatures
  and SSH: an absolute path, `~/…`, `SSH_AUTH_SOCK` or `none`
  (`rules/ssh-agent.md`).
- `Git signing setup: existing|configured|later` — written once by
  the signing interview, so it is not asked again
  (`rules/ssh-agent.md` → Optional signing setup).

# SSH Users

Default: your-username

## Per-server overrides

Optional. Add a `- hostname.example.com: different-username`
line here for each host that needs a different user than the
default.

# SSH Ports

Optional. `Alternative SSH ports: 52222, 2222` — the ports you
run sshd on instead of 22, tried in this order when port 22 of a
new host refuses the connection (`rules/ssh-config.md` → Finding
the Port).

# SSH Passwords

Only for a host that takes no SSH key. `bin/hostwarden-password`
writes a line per login, `- admin@rtr1.example.com: local keychain`
or `- admin@rtr1.example.com: 1password op://Hostwarden/rtr1/password`,
saying where the password comes from, never the password itself
(`rules/ssh-passwords.md`).
